[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6676"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Message From Andromeda</a>
(credits and magazine scores).

[Fact:first:Interceptor's First Spectrum Game]
Crash described Message From Andromeda as Interceptor's first Spectrum
game, after the company had made its name on the Commodore 64. It was coded
by David M. Banner, with in-game graphics by Jean A. Banner and the inlay by
Terry Greer, and cost &pound;5.50. Sources: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6676"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Crash review</a>,
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6676"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:interceptor:Interceptor Micros]
Interceptor was run by father and son Julian and Richard Jones from
Tadley in Hampshire. It was set up after a partnership with Jeff Minter
ended in 1982, and later added the budget labels Players and Players
Premier and the premium label Pandora. See
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pandora_(Interceptor_Software)"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Wikipedia</a>.

[Fact:pictures:Instant Pictures]
Crash noticed that the pictures appear almost at once because they are
stored ready-made rather than drawn line by line, and that commands are
typed in silence with no cursor. Crash also found that "SHOOT SOLDIER" fails
and you have to type "SHOOT ALIEN SOLDIER", and that dead soldiers stay where
they fell. See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6676"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Crash review</a>.

[Fact:earlscourt:Help From The Producer]
The Crash reviewer admitted getting the solution to the control panel from
Richard Paul Jones of Interceptor at the Earls Court Computer Fair. See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6676"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Crash review</a>.

[Fact:scores:Reviews]
Crash issue 7 gave it 7/10 and Big K issue 10 gave it 1/3. Source:
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6676"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:deepspace:Rooms Start At Two]
The room description table at #R$8568 leaves rooms #N$00 and #N$01 empty,
and the game starts in room #N($02), "In Deep Space,on patrol." (#R$7160),
which is set at #R$F8AF(#N$F8E2). The current room is only ever written at
#R$8E18(#N$8E2B), and nothing passes it room #N$00 or #N$01.
#FOREST$D44C(Forest At World's End) also leaves rooms #N$00 and #N$01 empty,
but starts in room #N$05.
